WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.741
Picture a hospital ward at night.

2
00:00:04.741 --> 00:00:10.308
Beside each bed, a small screen shows the patient's heart rate and temperature, right now.

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:11.187
At 3 a.m.

4
00:00:11.187 --> 00:00:13.512
a doctor asks a simple question.

5
00:00:13.512 --> 00:00:16.200
When did the fever in bed four start?

6
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:17.965
The screen cannot say.

7
00:00:17.965 --> 00:00:19.775
It only knows the present.

8
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.385
So a nurse walks the ward every fifteen minutes, and writes every number on a chart, with the time and the bed.

9
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.724
Now the doctor has an answer: between one fifteen and one thirty.

10
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:33.596
Our app has the same blind spot.

11
00:00:33.596 --> 00:00:39.525
hello-devops, the tiny lab app that greets every visitor, keeps a count of visits in memory.

12
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:43.428
It shows its counts on a plain web page, at slash metrics.

13
00:00:43.428 --> 00:00:45.912
That page is the screen beside the bed.

14
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:48.350
It always shows the value right now.

15
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.181
Nothing remembers what it was ten minutes ago.

16
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:58.553
Prometheus is the nurse with the chart: a program that collects numbers from your programs every few seconds.

17
00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.256
It collects by pulling.

18
00:01:00.256 --> 00:01:03.689
It walks to the app and fetches the page, on a timer.

19
00:01:03.689 --> 00:01:05.891
In the lab, every fifteen seconds.

20
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.894
One fetch is called a scrape.

21
00:01:07.894 --> 00:01:11.297
The thing it scrapes is a target, one bed on the round.

22
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.274
After each scrape it writes the value down with a timestamp.

23
00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:18.041
That history is what you graph and alert on.

24
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:25.145
Four scrapes, one minute of history.

25
00:01:25.145 --> 00:01:26.797
Now we can see the past.

26
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.107
Now the app crashes, and stops answering.

27
00:01:30.107 --> 00:01:32.092
How does Prometheus find out?

28
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.274
Its next scrape fails.

29
00:01:37.274 --> 00:01:41.639
And after every scrape, Prometheus records a value called up.

30
00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:43.356
One if the fetch worked.

31
00:01:43.356 --> 00:01:44.644
Zero if it failed.

32
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:48.019
A nurse who finds an empty bed writes no reading.

33
00:01:48.019 --> 00:01:51.172
A patient who has left cannot phone in to say so.

34
00:01:51.172 --> 00:01:56.641
Because Prometheus knows which targets should exist, silence itself becomes a signal.

35
00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:58.343
Two more gifts.

36
00:01:58.343 --> 00:02:01.404
You can read the same page yourself, with curl.

37
00:02:01.404 --> 00:02:04.725
And a slow monitoring server never blocks your app.

38
00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:06.553
Pushing still has a place.

39
00:02:06.553 --> 00:02:10.290
A batch job may finish in twenty seconds, before any scrape.

40
00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.529
Such jobs can leave their result at the Pushgateway.

41
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:16.762
A real page has more lines.

42
00:02:16.762 --> 00:02:21.119
Here is one metric, hello responses total, shown three times.

43
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:25.933
The part in braces is labels, a name and a value, like status is 500.

44
00:02:25.933 --> 00:02:30.733
A label says which part of the app a number is about, like a bed number.

45
00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:33.204
Same name, three label sets.

46
00:02:33.204 --> 00:02:35.556
How many rows does the chart need?

47
00:02:35.556 --> 00:02:36.663
Pause and guess.

48
00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:39.627
Three rows.

49
00:02:39.627 --> 00:02:44.120
Each exact combination of name and label values gets its own row.

50
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.783
Change any label value, and you have a different row.

51
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.401
Each scrape adds one sample, a timestamp and a value, to every row.

52
00:02:52.633 --> 00:02:54.369
That row has a name now.

53
00:02:54.369 --> 00:02:59.068
A time series: a metric name, plus one exact set of label values.

54
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:06.133
Prometheus adds two labels of its own: job, the name from its config, and instance, the target's address.

55
00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:07.889
So far so good.

56
00:03:07.889 --> 00:03:10.357
Now let's break it with one word.

57
00:03:10.357 --> 00:03:11.779
A label looks free.

58
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.771
Count what one route already costs.

59
00:03:14.771 --> 00:03:20.848
Its timing histogram has fifteen buckets, plus a sum and a count, and the counters add a few more.

60
00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:28.647
In the lab, one request to a route nobody had called, slash ready Z, took the series from forty four to sixty four.

61
00:03:28.867 --> 00:03:32.898
Now someone adds a user ID label, to see requests per user.

62
00:03:32.898 --> 00:03:34.880
There are ten thousand users.

63
00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.929
How many series for one route?

64
00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:41.736
Twenty times ten thousand.

65
00:03:41.736 --> 00:03:46.255
Two hundred thousand series, for one route, from one copy of the app.

66
00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:49.442
The whole lab holds about two thousand two hundred.

67
00:03:49.442 --> 00:03:53.819
One careless label makes this app about ninety times bigger than all of it.

68
00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:57.617
It is the hospital adding a chart row for every visitor.

69
00:03:57.617 --> 00:03:59.280
Nobody notices on day one.

70
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.352
By the end of the month, the chart room is full.

71
00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:04.734
That number has a name.

72
00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:08.210
Cardinality: how many time series a metric creates.

73
00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:11.891
Every new combination of label values is a new series.

74
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:17.888
Labels hold small, fixed sets of values: the status code, the method, the route pattern.

75
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:23.291
Never user IDs, email addresses, request IDs or raw URLs.

76
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.416
The lab records the route pattern, not the address typed.

77
00:04:27.416 --> 00:04:32.431
So slash nope and slash admin dot P H P both become one value: unmatched.

78
00:04:32.633 --> 00:04:36.065
Details about one request belong in logs and traces.

79
00:04:36.065 --> 00:04:40.156
The guide's rule of thumb: keep most metrics under ten series.

80
00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:42.374
And one honest limit.

81
00:04:42.374 --> 00:04:47.185
Prometheus keeps its chart on one machine's disk, in a single copy.

82
00:04:47.185 --> 00:04:48.908
Fifteen days by default.

83
00:04:48.908 --> 00:04:50.344
The lab keeps seven.

84
00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:52.814
So the moral of the story.

85
00:04:52.814 --> 00:04:57.181
A label with no fixed list of values is a leak with a slow start.

86
00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.779
The page shows now, and the rounds write history.

87
00:05:00.779 --> 00:05:02.916
An empty bed is up equals zero.

88
00:05:02.916 --> 00:05:05.399
And every new label value adds rows.

89
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.058
A quick check.

90
00:05:07.058 --> 00:05:12.601
A counter has a label method, with two values, and a label path, with two values.

91
00:05:12.601 --> 00:05:14.312
At most, how many series?

92
00:05:17.433 --> 00:05:17.882
Four.

93
00:05:17.882 --> 00:05:19.140
Two times two.

94
00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.104
Every combination is its own row.
